---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ScrollTable from '../../components/ScrollTable.astro';
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
---

<Guide
  title="Server rendering"
  description="Version 1 renders components in the browser only (DECISIONS D6). Static markup works anywhere; the elements and the React components need a client-only spot."
>
  <h2 id="what">What runs where</h2>
  <ScrollTable label="What runs where">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">On the server</th><th scope="col">In the browser</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">CSS classes</th>
          <td>Render anywhere: they are plain HTML and CSS.</td>
          <td>Complete without JavaScript; <code>ripple.js</code> adds the press ripple.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Custom elements</th>
          <td>
            Importing an entry is safe: registration is skipped where there is no
            <code>window</code>. The tag is output as written.
          </td>
          <td>The element renders its markup when its entry loads.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">React</th>
          <td>Not supported: no server rendering or React Server Components.</td>
          <td>Mount the components on the client.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ScrollTable>
  <p>
    The React components carry no <code>'use client'</code> directive. Nothing here is meant to be
    rendered on the server, so mount them from a module of your own that only runs in the browser.
  </p>

  <h2 id="astro">Astro</h2>
  <p>
    Mount React components with <code>client:only="react"</code>. The other client directives
    render on the server first. See the <a href="/get-started/astro/">Astro guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">Next.js (App Router)</h2>
  <p>
    Wrap the components in your own client component, and load that component without server
    rendering:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="tsx"
    label="Next.js client wrapper"
    code={`// app/toolbar-client.tsx
'use client';

import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

export const Toolbar = dynamic(() => import('./toolbar'), { ssr: false });`}
  />
  <CodeBlock
    lang="tsx"
    label="Next.js toolbar module"
    code={`// app/toolbar.tsx: only ever loaded in the browser
import { FloatingToolbar, Icon, IconButton } from '@kyralabs/m3exp-web/react';

export default function Toolbar() {
  return (
    <FloatingToolbar aria-label="Formatting">
      <IconButton aria-label="Bold"><Icon name="format_bold" /></IconButton>
      <IconButton aria-label="Italic"><Icon name="format_italic" /></IconButton>
    </FloatingToolbar>
  );
}`}
  />
  <p>
    Import the CSS once in the root layout (<code>app/layout.tsx</code>):
    <code>import '@kyralabs/m3exp-web/css/index.css';</code>. Pages can still server-render the
    m3e-css markup around the client-only parts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="other">Other frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The same rule applies everywhere: server-render the static markup if you like, and create the
    elements and React components only in the browser (a client-only route, a lazy import after
    mount, or the framework's equivalent of <code>ssr: false</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Server rendering may come in a later version; until then, D6 keeps the components free of
    server-specific code.
  </p>
</Guide>
